From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 07:59:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: Make the disk the primary HTTP cache

Add `DiskStore`, a file-per-entry store behind `CacheStore`, and use it
for the public cache whenever the embedder gives Servo a cache
directory. Cached bodies then live only on disk, the page cache of the
system is the memory tier, and the net process holds an index.

The layout follows Chromium's Simple Cache: a version stamp whose
mismatch discards the store's files, an index written through a
temporary file and a rename, and one file per variant under `entries/`,
written under `tmp/` until it is committed, without any fsync:

    magic u64 | format u32 | flags u32
    body, exactly as received
    meta, postcard(EntryMeta)
    meta_len u32 | meta_crc u32 | body_crc u32 | body_len u64 | eof magic u64

The metadata comes after the body, so freshening after a 304 truncates
and appends instead of rewriting the body. A file without a valid
trailer was never committed and is ignored. The body checksum is checked
on a read to the end, not on a ranged read. The format includes the
version of the serialized `StoredPolicy` of `http-cache-rules`, so a
change of the crate's format discards old entries.

The index holds key hash, slot, size and last use per entry; headers
and policy are read from the entry file on lookup. A bad checksum, a
missing file or a directory that changed while Servo was not running
repair by scanning, and a directory the system replaces while the store
runs, found out by a write or an index write, starts the index over
empty rather than keep counting, and later persist, entries that are
gone. That is what lets the store survive OpenHarmony emptying
`cacheDir` at any time. An entry whose body does not read back is
unlinked. A commit renames its file and records it in one job, so an
index write never sees one without the other.

The store locks its directory (`flock`, or a handle without sharing on
Windows) while it is open. A second process on the same directory, such
as another servoshell, falls back to the memory store instead of
overwriting the first one's entry files; servoshell gives a profile
chosen with `--config-dir` a directory of its own. Under the lock,
opening the store empties `tmp/`, so the file of a writer killed after
an index write does not stay behind. An `index` file that the store did
not write makes it refuse the directory rather than delete anything,
and a stamp of another format of the store removes only the store's own
files.

All blocking I/O goes to one thread per store. Batches of up to 256 KiB
are handed over, since a cross-thread hand-off costs 28 to 45 us on the
DAYU200 and the PLR, several times a 64 KiB read.

Sizing: 100 MiB on OpenHarmony and Android, Chromium's free-space ladder
elsewhere, both overridable through `network_http_disk_cache_size`.
Entries are capped at an eighth of the budget, and at 20 000 files.
`Opts::http_cache_dir` carries the location; servoshell defaults it to
`<config dir>/http-cache` on Android and OpenHarmony and to
`dirs::cache_dir()/servo/http-cache` elsewhere. `--temporary-storage`
and private browsing stay on the memory store, so WPT runs are
unaffected.

`about:memory` reports `http-cache/index` for the in-process index and
a non-heap `http-cache/disk` for the bytes on disk.

The index is written 20 s after the last change, Chromium's
`kWriteToDiskDelayMSecs`. A phone application is frozen and then killed
rather than shut down, so `NetworkManager::notify_application_backgrounded`
is a new embedder API that writes it at once and returns when it is
written, as Chromium does on `APPLICATION_STATE_HAS_STOPPED_ACTIVITIES`.
A dirty flag in the version stamp, Firefox's approach, says whether the
previous run ended cleanly, and the directory's mtime whether the system
emptied it meanwhile; the index is only trusted if both hold. The log
says whether the index was loaded or rebuilt by scanning, since the two
behave alike otherwise.

Fork note: from the `http-cache-v2` branch, but for the version of the
entry format and the entry stamps of patch 120: the store reads the
metadata of an entry file before acting on it, and leaves the file alone
if it holds another stamp.

Testing: tests/cache_store.rs runs one suite against both stores, and a
hostile suite against the disk store: restart, corrupt index, a left
temporary file, an entry file or the whole directory removed while the
store runs, then flushed and reopened, a stamp of another format and an
`index` file of something else, a second store on the directory, a
writer in flight at an index write and a kill, a flipped body byte, a
forged hash collision, an outsized entry, and no credential of the
request or of the URL in the entry file.

Validation: `./mach test-unit -p servo-net`: 481 tests pass. Negative controls, each run once: without the lock, emptying `tmp/` on open, the check for a replaced directory, refusing a foreign `index` file, or the password left out of the key, `a_second_store_cannot_open_a_directory_in_use`, `a_writer_in_flight_when_the_index_is_flushed_leaves_nothing_behind`, `entries_removed_while_the_store_runs_are_not_kept_in_its_index` (with `the_whole_directory_can_be_removed_while_the_store_runs`), `a_directory_with_a_foreign_index_file_is_left_alone` and `credentials_are_never_written_to_an_entry` fail. Clean with `-D warnings` on Linux and OHOS at patch 123. On the PLR-AL50 (production HAP, `--flavor harmonyos`), with patch 122: a page of 8 cacheable resources, its HTML, CSS and JS gzip-encoded, loads after a force-stop and restart without a server request, 5 of 5 runs, and the directory holds `entries/`, an empty `tmp/`, `lock`, `index` and `index-data`. On the build before the review fixes of patches 120 and 121: the entry files hold the bodies as received, and PSS 30 s after fetching and discarding 40 MiB of bodies, 3 alternating pairs against ohos-main 013edabb25d, is 291.4 MB on ohos-main and 252.2 MB here (-39.2 MB, -13.4%), with no overlap between the runs of the two builds.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent wrote this on the http-cache-v2 branch, and ported it with the version of the entry format of http-cache-rules)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
